### A.5: Fix Login Page — Replace Client-Side Cookie Synthesis with PKCE Flow
**Goal:** Eliminate the XSS-exploitable, 400-day client-side session cookie hack and use the proper server-side auth callback.

**Current State — CRITICAL SECURITY GAP:**
- [login/page.tsx](file:///c:/Application%20V4.0/src/app/login/page.tsx) L41–97: manually writes Supabase session cookies via `document.cookie` with `max-age=34560000` (400 days!)
- Bypasses `httpOnly` — session token is readable by any XSS payload
- Reimplements `@supabase/ssr` cookie chunking logic — fragile

**Changes:**
1. **[MODIFY]** `src/app/login/page.tsx`:
   - Change SSO redirect URL from `/login` to `/auth/callback` (PKCE flow)
   - Remove the entire 60-line `onAuthStateChange` cookie synthesis block (L30–118)
   - Parameterize `providerId` from env var (see A.6)
   - Keep the SSO button and auto-initiate logic
2. **[MODIFY]** `src/app/auth/callback/route.ts` — verify it correctly handles SSO PKCE redirects (already calls `exchangeCodeForSession` — should work)

> [!WARNING]
> Requires testing: the original cookie synthesis was a workaround for GoTrue 504 timeouts. If PKCE flow also 504s, fallback plan is a thin server API route that calls `setSession()` and sets `httpOnly` cookies.

**Acceptance Criteria:**
- [ ] SSO login completes via `/auth/callback` server-side — no `document.cookie` writes
- [ ] Session cookies are `httpOnly` (not readable by JS)
- [ ] Cookie max-age follows JWT expiry (30 min), not 400 days

